# WooCommerce Rules

## HPOS Only (ADR-004)

- HPOS is enabled from day one. NEVER read/write orders via `postmeta`,
  `get_post_meta( $order_id )`, or direct posts-table queries.
- Always use CRUD objects: `wc_get_order()`, `WC_Order`, `WC_Product`,
  `$order->get_meta()` / `$order->update_meta_data()` / `$order->save()`.
- Declare HPOS compatibility in every custom plugin:
  `FeaturesUtil::declare_compatibility( 'custom_order_tables', ... )`.

## Products

- Use WooCommerce product types (simple, variable) for physical goods and for
  Bingo Play Products (BR-039).
- Portal owns Game Type identity/configuration, keyed across systems by Portal
  `game_types.id`; BingoCommerce retains a hall-scoped local Game Type replica.
  Bingo Play Product metadata (Medium, Faces per unit, Game Type reference) and
  Bingo Package definitions/components/add-on eligibility remain authoritative
  BingoCommerce-owned structured domain data (BR-037/BR-039/BR-040/BR-061/
  BR-062/BR-071/BR-072, ADR-026/ADR-031/ADR-035). Runtime logic must never infer
  them from a WooCommerce product title, SKU, tag, category, or naming convention.
  ADR-031's per-site Catalog tables are authoritative; linked WooCommerce
  products are commerce shells. A WooCommerce product ID must not be linked
  simultaneously as a Package and Bingo Play Product. Enforcement must be
  concurrency-safe; a standalone check-then-insert across the authoritative
  tables is insufficient. The approved ADR-032/ADR-033 serialization and
  transaction mechanisms are implemented; preserve their reviewed boundaries
  and do not add a table or automatically reuse ADR-025.
- The linked WooCommerce Package product's native price is the Package base-
  price authority. ADR-044/BR-079 approve `bingo_package_addons` as the authority
  for Package-specific add-on price, 1–99 maximum quantity, enabled state and
  exact eligibility. Catalog v6 and the backend `PackageAddonService` are
  implemented; Slice 3 implements Package Admin UI/request handling.
  The customer cart backend and electronic-limit validation at cart addition
  and classic/Store API checkout are implemented. The customer storefront theme
  and selection screens remain pending; no real Hall migration or deployment is
  claimed.
  When preparing an eligible never-configured row after WooCommerce
  initialization and outside the Catalog transaction, load
  `\wc_get_product( $wc_product_id )`, require `\WC_Product`, and treat
  `$product->get_regular_price( 'edit' )` as a string-only editable prefill. Do
  not use `get_price()`, sale price, direct `_regular_price` post meta, formatted
  HTML, tax-adjusted display price, filtered `view` context, or floats. During
  form preparation only, returned strings `5` and `5.5` may be padded without
  rounding to `5.00` and `5.50`; the displayed/submitted field has exactly two
  fractional digits. Invalid values or values requiring rounding leave the field
  blank; prefill persists nothing. Existing relationships always show their
  saved price and are never refreshed from WooCommerce.

  A Manager/service price must already be a canonical non-negative
  `DECIMAL(19,2)` string with exactly two fractional digits. Reject `5`, `5.0`,
  `5.5`, excess precision, exponent notation, signs, symbols, commas, malformed
  values, noncanonical leading zeros and overflow; never normalize, convert
  through floating point or round them. `PackageAddonService::create()` receives
  that submitted canonical string and never reads WooCommerce pricing in
  mutation preflight or the transaction. Later regular- or sale-price changes
  never rewrite the saved relationship price. Component-product prices are never
  summed into Package price.
- Under BR-065, BR-032's `bc_product_kind` and `bc_package_relation` are
  required derived compatibility/cart-enforcement projections maintained by
  Catalog services, not independent authorities. Projection updates must be deterministic,
  idempotent, verifiable, and reconcilable through WooCommerce CRUD. Do not
  invent a direct-SQL transaction spanning Catalog rows and product metadata,
  and do not automatically reuse ADR-025's Hall Staff transaction transport.
- ADR-044 approves one fixed add-on mutation profile within the existing
  Catalog transport. Audit and WooCommerce CRUD/projection remain outside it.
  Bulk administration calls one relationship operation at a time; no bulk
  transaction is permitted. A failed projection leaves the Catalog mutation
  committed and is surfaced for conditional synchronization retry. Targeted
  retry accepts `bingo_play_product_id` and resolves its linked current-Hall
  WooCommerce product internally; never trust a request-supplied WooCommerce
  product ID. The Hall-wide mismatch scanner is deferred until the later
  storefront/readiness phase and must not become a normal Manager dashboard.
- The Package editor shows every existing configured relationship, including
  disabled or currently ineligible rows. Never-configured candidates are active,
  medium-compatible current-Hall Play Products only. The section is one form
  with one `Save Add-ons` action; unchecked never-configured rows are ignored
  without price/maximum validation or any write. Remove uses simple browser
  confirmation only: cancel sends no request, confirm submits the row operation.
- Membership in `bingo_play_products` remains authoritative and keeps that
  product non-independently-purchasable in v1 even if compatibility metadata
  says `bc_product_kind = regular` and `bc_package_relation = none`.
- An immutable purchase-time entitlement snapshot is created when online
  payment is successfully confirmed (BR-043, BR-053): later product/package/
  Game Type edits must never alter what a customer already bought. Do not
  hardcode a particular WooCommerce order-status name as the business rule.

## Cart & Checkout

- One cart and one order belong to exactly ONE hall (BR-003). Because each hall
  is its own Multisite site, this is native — never write code that merges or
  references another site's cart/products/orders.
- Availability rules (calendar-dated Session, 30-day sales window, Available
  for Sale ON/OFF) are enforced authoritatively server-side at pre-payment
  commerce boundaries: display, add-to-cart, cart update, checkout, and the
  appropriate pre-payment/payment-initiation boundary. Front-end hiding alone
  is never authorization. A Session appears for purchase only
  when `session_date - 30 calendar days <= hall-local today <= session_date`,
  using the applicable hall/schedule timezone, AND Available for Sale = ON
  (BR-051/BR-063/BR-077). A schedule-managed occurrence must also belong to
  the schedule's current committed definition (BR-078). Removing a strictly
  future date immediately makes it ineligible and a successful edit turns it
  OFF; re-adding a retained occurrence leaves it OFF until explicitly enabled.
  New Sessions default to Available for Sale OFF.
  The customer selects the Session before the Bingo Package (BR-052). Do not
  implement week/month future-Session browsing as a requirement, and do not
  invent automatic open/close times from text in the Session name (BR-050).
  Do not decide here how to resolve a payment that was initiated while eligible
  but confirmed after the calendar/availability boundary; that gateway race
  belongs to the later checkout/payment integration design. BR-053 remains in
  force: successful online payment confirmation activates entitlement.
- For Portal-integrated commerce, the selectable Session is the exact
  BingoCommerce dated occurrence generated from a BingoCommerce commerce
  schedule linked to a Portal-owned Session template (BR-073/ADR-035). Portal
  Session `days` must not drive Commerce scheduling, generate occurrences, or
  constrain/validate Commerce scheduling in v1; later advisory validation
  requires a separately approved requirement. Received/shared Sessions are
  excluded. The schedule requires structured Commerce time, which a Hall
  Manager may enter manually in v1; display-name text remains non-authoritative.
  One Package may be assigned to multiple dated occurrences. The compatibility
  review is complete: the additive Portal-template, Commerce-schedule and
  dated-occurrence model is implemented under ADR-043, preserving the legacy
  Session tables and relationships.
- At most one Bingo Package per checkout, plus its eligible add-ons and any
  ordinary merchandise (BR-032, BR-040) — never more than one package.
- Final Package identity and Package/add-on eligibility checks use authoritative
  Catalog services/tables. Metadata may assist compatibility routing but cannot
  override Catalog state or identify which Package an add-on is eligible for.
- The electronic-card limit is validated with authoritative server-side
  checks at checkout, aggregated per Game Type against the current effective
  hall limit (BR-038, BR-042) — see the database rule file. Checkout does not
  increment or reserve a shared Session-wide counter, so it needs no
  transactional locking for that purpose (that atomicity requirement applies
  to Android redemption accumulation, BR-045, not checkout). There is no
  session-wide order-capacity check.

## Payments

- Use WooCommerce's pluggable payment gateway API (ADR-010). Never hardcode
  a specific provider. Gateway-specific behavior stays behind an adapter.
- Verify payment webhooks; protect against replay and duplicate delivery.
  Repeated webhook delivery must never duplicate charges, orders, stock
  changes, or emails.
- For online Bingo Package purchases, entitlement/QR activation occurs only
  after successful payment confirmation (BR-053). Failed, cancelled,
  or unsuccessful payment must not create an active Bingo entitlement or QR.
  Do not hardcode a particular WooCommerce status name as the business rule.

## Orders & Refunds

- Order state, physical fulfillment state, and digital entitlement state are
  separate concepts — keep them separate in code and UI.
- Refunding a Bingo Package voids its digital QR entitlement (BR-044) and is
  refunded as a whole — no partial refund of a package or its package-scoped
  add-ons (BR-046). Ordinary merchandise in the same order remains
  independently refundable per hall policy. Refund handling is separate from
  Android redemption-limit enforcement (BR-045).
